<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Starving Time</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1609">1609</a>–<a href="/years/1610">1610</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Starving Time was the winter of 1609–1610 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, when most of the English colonists died of famine and disease. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> cite it as evidence of the precariousness of early English colonisation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Jamestown nonetheless survived.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/starving-time.jpg" alt="Starving Time" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Beary (via Royall Bascom Smithey) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sickness_at_Jamestown_pp41_historyofvirgini00smith_0047_1898.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The first settlers, 104 men and boys brought by Captain Christopher Newport in 1607, chose a site that was easy to defend but had poor hunting and little drinking water. They never planned to grow all their own food and relied on trade with the Powhatan and on supply ships from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, while conflicts with the Powhatan kept them behind their walls. In August 1609 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-smith-explorer">John Smith</a>, the leader most able to trade with the Powhatans, was injured in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> accident and left for England, and the Powhatans stopped trading.</p><p>The third supply, a fleet of nine ships carrying 500 new colonists, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 2 June 1609. A storm, possibly a hurricane, drove the flagship Sea Venture onto the reefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bermuda">Bermuda</a> with most of the supplies and the colony's leaders, Sir George Somers and Sir Thomas Gates. Seven ships reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> with 200–300 more people but few supplies, after a drought had left the fields barren.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Over the winter the colonists traded arms and tools to the Powhatans for a little food, burned houses for firewood and ate laundry starch; archaeologists have found evidence that they ate cats, dogs, horses and rats. Of about 500 people at the start of winter, only about 60 were alive in the spring. George Percy, president of the council during the crisis, later reported that the living dug up and ate corpses and that a husband killed and ate his wife; the man was executed. In 2013 forensic study of the butchered remains of a girl of about fourteen, named Jane by the excavators, confirmed cannibalism in at least one case.</p><p>The Sea Venture's survivors built two small ships, Deliverance and Patience, and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> on 23 May 1610 to find the fort in ruins. The colony was abandoned on 7 June, but the new governor, Thomas West, Baron De La Warr, met the departing ships near Mulberry Island and forced them back.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Among those turned back was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-rolfe">John Rolfe</a>, who in 1611 is credited with first cultivating sweeter strains of tobacco commercially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>; exports from 1612 made the colony profitable. Lord Delaware's force began a counter-offensive against the Powhatan Confederacy, and Rolfe's marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pocahontas">Pocahontas</a> in 1614 brought a short truce. By 1646 the colony had broken up the confederacy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Starving Time” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first permanent English settlement in the Americas came at Jamestown, Virginia, in 1607, a colony so precarious that the great majority of its settlers died during the “<b>Starving Time</b>” of 1609–1610.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Relations broke down, and in the First Anglo-Powhatan War (1609–1614) his warriors ambushed a party under John Ratcliffe and besieged Jamestown through the winter of 1609–1610, the <b>Starving Time</b>, in which most of the fort’s colonists died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/powhatan-confederacy">Powhatan Confederacy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Disease, hunger and conflict killed most early colonists, especially in the <b>Starving Time</b> of 1609 to 1610.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-virginia">Colony of Virginia</a></li></ul></section>
